      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Given that one edge of the cube is 6 inches, the volume of the cube can be found using the formula $V = s^3$, where $s$ represents the edge length of the cube. Hence, we have:\n\\[\nV = 6^3 = 6 \\times 6 \\times 6 = 216\n\\]\nThe largest sphere that can be inscribed in a cube has a diameter equal to the edge length of the cube. So, the radius $r$ of the sphere is half of the edge length of the cube, which is $\\frac{6}{2} = 3$ inches.\n\nSince the volume $V$ of a sphere is given by the formula $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$, we can find the volume of the inscribed sphere by substituting the radius:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3)^3.\n\\]\nTo give the answer in cubic inches, we plug in the values and simplify:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (27) = \\frac{4 \\times 27}{3}\\pi = 36\\pi\n\\]\nThus, the volume of the inscribed sphere is $\\boxed{36\\pi}$ cubic inches.\n\nWe have a cube with an edge length of 6 inches. To find the volume of the cube, it's important to understand that the volume $V$ of a cube is given by $V = s^3$, where $s$ is the edge length of the cube.\n\nTherefore, we substitute the edge length ($s$) with 6 in the formula to find the volume of the cube:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{cube}} = 6^3 = 216\n\\]\nHowever, we know that the sphere is inscribed within the cube, which means the diameter of the sphere is equal to the edge length of the cube.\n\nThe radius $r$, therefore, is half of the edge length, so $r = \\frac{6}{2} = 3$ inches.\n\nTo calculate the volume of the inscribed sphere, we use the formula $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (3)^3\n\\]\n\nSince $(3)^3 = 27$ and $27$ is divisible by $3$, we can simplify the expression:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4 \\times 27}{3} \\pi\n\\]\n\nCalculating with numbers:\n\\[\nV_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\times 27 = 36 \\pi\n\\]\n\nSo, the volume of the inscribed sphere, given in cubic inches with $\\pi$, is $\\boxed{36\\pi}$.",
